# ConnectIt Brainstorm

Currently struggling trying to coordinate all the systems when a game event happens.
Many systems seem to be boxing then unboxing events. There lacks a coherent flow and consistency.

Instead of various 'systems' (such as interpreters) firing independently or trying to work separately
we can have a coordinator class (like gameplay ability class) which is given all require systems
and then organizes them appropriately, waiting for callbacks, etc.

We would create a subclass for each specific game event (very similar to gameplay ability system...)

EVENT WALKTHROUGH OF PIECE SCORING
Piece Placed - piece animation
Line Scored - scoring pieces animate, tiles animate when adjusting multiplier, pieces are removed with animation





REWIRING (Board State Updated)
It all begins with the AConnectIt_BoardManager::ProcessRequest() trying to be generic.
Requests should be individual so that parameters can be customized. This makes sense given the 
current setup as we are passing the request up the chain from the action to the action component
and then to the player controller. 

ProcessRequest() is where the board interprets valid actions.
It might be a lot easier or make more sense if, instead of just processing
the single request, updating the board and setting a change event.
We instead process the event, then check for all other potential events
such as line score, or multiplier changed or game over, etc.
Create a list of specific events (not using a generic) then replicate that info
down with the new board state?

By shoving everything in here FConnectItBoardChangeEvent it does not necessarily
explain what needs to happen first or when.
We need to devise a structure for checking what happens first then
executing the visuals before moving onto the next thing.


(Pre Process Request *still not content with this setup)->
Process Request (gets info by gameplay tag and routes to handle requests)->
Handle Request needs to properly create a BoardChangeEvent then broadcast new board state ->


GridPieceRegistry
Should have GetPiece, RemovePiece
For getpiece if there isn't a piece mapped to the requested gridposition, it should
check that a piece at the grid position is valid according to the board state
if it is, it should get the correct actor and instantiate it appropriately
then return the actor

same goes for remove piece, if there should be a piece at grid position according
to the board state then don't do anything?

starting to confuse myself around why we need the grid registry? What was it's purpose again?

Really when responding to the GameEvent we only need a single actor taking charge and 
coordinating all the possible sub events in a logical sequential way that works for the game...


Create Game Event BP classes
Where should the concrete BP classes live? Get constructed? GameBoardManager?


SEPT 2, 2026

Grid tiles and pieces no longer register themselves with the grid world subsystem. This responsibility
should lie with the piece and tile registries we will interface with both the grid subsystem and the actor pool subsystem.
Grid subsystem should be renamed to GridHoverSubsytem. The responsibility of the subsystem is to simply
relay when a tile or a piece THAT IS REGISTERED WITH IT, NOT ALL TILES OR PIECES BECAUSE OF THE ACTOR POOL
are hovered over and to broadcast this. This will help create a generic high level flow for piece creation, pooling and game tracking.

Registries are responsible for tracking real actors to grid positions as well initializing actors
when needed by using the pool and referencing values from the BoardState. The initialization because it 
could potentially deal with BP classes should be Blueprint Implementable.
Registeries should be converted to UObjects instead of ActorComponents to support polymorphism and BP creation.
Each registry will be different for each project but provide the same useful high level functionality.

BoardManager should be renamed to GameBoard or GameBoardVisualizer to better explain it's purpose 
which is to read the valid data board state and create the concrete visual board in the world.
Because of this distinction BoardState should probably live on the GameState (like the turn manager) rather than the BoardManager. 
It is relatively light weight so replication should not be an issue.
We can create in interface for concrete implementations to grab required information without having to cast.

Instead of the player controller reaching into the game board to process a request. We should have something
listening to the player controller broadcasting a ProcessRequest delegate with the same payload.
We could even have the game mode handle this potentially (unless we want swappable logic for each request for testing...)
